gcc/config/i386/cygming.h's own USER_LABEL_PREFIX -- the string cc1
prepends to every C-level symbol it emits (function names, global
variable names) -- defaults to "_" for a 32-bit target, the traditional
PE/COFF convention real mingw/cygwin runtimes actually export their
symbols under.

ntlibc is not a port of the mingw/cygwin runtime -- it is a from-scratch
POSIX libc, and its own real symbol names are plain and undecorated,
checked directly with nm against the actual built libc.a and crt1.o:
libc.a exports `write` (not `_write`), and crt1.o's own startup code
calls `main` (not `_main`) to invoke a compiled program's entry point.
Left at the real GCC default, this package's own functional test (a
plain hello.c compiled by this chain's own cc1.exe, then linked by this
chain's own tcc against ntlibc) fails at link time wanting `_write`,
`main` (present, but with `_main`'s callers looking for the wrong name)
and `___main` (see the companion fix in this same commit, libgcc's own
__main stub -- also affected by this same prefix) -- none of which
ntlibc actually provides under those decorated names.

TARGET_64BIT's own ternary is dropped rather than preserved (unlike
LOCAL_LABEL_PREFIX just below it, left alone): this bootstrap is
32-bit-only (i686-pc-pe), so the 64-bit branch is dead code here either
way, and leaving a live ternary that still names a real convention (the
64-bit branch's own "") would misstate why the 32-bit branch changed --
it isn't becoming "like 64-bit', it's becoming "like ntlibc's own real
ABI", a genuinely different reason.

--- gcc/config/i386/cygming.h
+++ gcc/config/i386/cygming.h
@@ -55,7 +55,16 @@

 #if ! defined (USE_MINGW64_LEADING_UNDERSCORES)
 #undef USER_LABEL_PREFIX
-#define USER_LABEL_PREFIX (TARGET_64BIT ? "" : "_")
+/* nova-nix Windows bootstrap: real mingw/cygwin runtimes export their
+   32-bit C symbols with a leading underscore (the traditional PE/COFF
+   convention); ntlibc does not -- it is a from-scratch POSIX libc with
+   plain, undecorated names (`write`, `main`, ... -- checked directly
+   with nm against both ntlibc's own libc.a and crt1.o, see
+   user-label-prefix.patch's own header). Left at "_" for 32-bit, every
+   symbol cc1 emits for a call into ntlibc or into a target program's own
+   main() fails to link. "" unconditionally: TARGET_64BIT is not a real
+   possibility for this 32-bit-only bootstrap anyway. */
+#define USER_LABEL_PREFIX ""

 #undef LOCAL_LABEL_PREFIX
 #define LOCAL_LABEL_PREFIX (TARGET_64BIT ? "." : "")
